1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:04,970
Something went wrong, and
someone was hurt. What happened,

2
00:00:05,020 --> 00:00:10,430
in what order, and why? Investigators
answer with an events and causal factors

3
00:00:10,480 --> 00:00:14,750
chart: the events in sequence,
the conditions beneath them,

4
00:00:14,800 --> 00:00:17,040
and the causal factors marked.

5
00:00:20,860 --> 00:00:25,990
A made-up case: on a warehouse night shift,
a forklift reversing out of an aisle

6
00:00:26,040 --> 00:00:31,260
strikes a picker crossing its end. Each
primary event is a moment at its exact

7
00:00:31,310 --> 00:00:37,020
time, from statements, badge
records, telematics and cameras.

8
00:00:37,070 --> 00:00:41,970
The collision is camera seven's frame,
nine and nine twenty-fifths seconds past

9
00:00:42,020 --> 00:00:46,550
two thirty-four, stored exactly.
Where accounts disagree,

10
00:00:46,600 --> 00:00:48,557
both stay, with notes.

11
00:00:50,337 --> 00:00:54,287
Conditions held over time: ten-hour
night shifts since Monday,

12
00:00:54,337 --> 00:00:58,237
the aisle-end light out
since Tuesday, no spotter.

13
00:00:58,287 --> 00:01:03,127
Each is a duration beneath the events,
all still there at the collision.

14
00:01:03,177 --> 00:01:08,045
And the collision's moment stack keeps
its conditions and evidence together.

15
00:01:09,785 --> 00:01:13,315
Relationships link each condition
and event to what it led to,

16
00:01:13,365 --> 00:01:17,975
drawn as arcs, with arrows
from cause to effect.

17
00:01:18,025 --> 00:01:21,075
Open the collision, choose
Separate all connected,

18
00:01:21,125 --> 00:01:23,645
and the chain behind it
lifts onto its own track,

19
00:01:23,695 --> 00:01:26,451
away from the routine records.

20
00:01:28,171 --> 00:01:31,011
Then the analysis, which
is the investigator's,

21
00:01:31,061 --> 00:01:36,791
not the software's. Give each causal
factor the Bad color and a causal factor

22
00:01:36,841 --> 00:01:42,681
tag, and the root cause one more. Write
the reasoning and its evidence in the

23
00:01:42,731 --> 00:01:46,651
notes. What isn't confirmed, like
the driver's fatigue or the horn,

24
00:01:46,701 --> 00:01:49,720
is marked presumptive, and drawn dashed.

25
00:01:51,360 --> 00:01:55,060
The chart is drawn the
way the method draws it.

26
00:01:55,110 --> 00:02:00,830
Conditions are ellipses. Presumptive
items have a dashed outline.

27
00:02:00,880 --> 00:02:05,470
And relationship arrows point
each link from cause to effect.

28
00:02:05,520 --> 00:02:08,900
The arrows are a drawing
convention kept on the events:

29
00:02:08,950 --> 00:02:13,100
a link has no label, like
caused or contributed to,

30
00:02:13,150 --> 00:02:16,234
and Separate all connected
doesn't follow them.

31
00:02:17,874 --> 00:02:23,064
Compare puts the written procedure on
the same axis as the night itself.

32
00:02:23,114 --> 00:02:26,164
As planned: an eight-hour
shift, a lighting walk,

33
00:02:26,214 --> 00:02:30,174
a spotter on duty, a break
at two. As it happened:

34
00:02:30,224 --> 00:02:33,709
a longer shift, no walk,
no spotter, no break.

35
00:02:37,284 --> 00:02:42,084
Then the review. A colleague proposes
corrections as a pull request,

36
00:02:42,134 --> 00:02:47,234
the lead merges it, and saved
history keeps every revision.

37
00:02:47,284 --> 00:02:53,234
Export .ochx for the report,
or keep a desktop .och file.

38
00:02:53,284 --> 00:02:57,084
Timelines start private, access
is given person by person,

39
00:02:57,134 --> 00:03:01,065
and a self-hosted server
keeps it in-house.

40
00:03:02,615 --> 00:03:06,695
Every investigation is a
timeline. Build it once,

41
00:03:06,745 --> 00:03:09,301
carefully, and let everyone check it.
